window.docContents[44].resolve({"/Subgraphs/#section-building-partial-colourings":{"contents":"Fundamentally we are interested in partial colourings as a tool to build total\ncolourings, so we want to be able to turn a G.ColoringOn γ Set.univ into a\nG.Coloring γ.\n\n/-- A G.ColoringOn γ Set.univ is a G.Coloring γ -/\ndef ColoringOn.toColoring (C : G.ColoringOn γ Set.univ) : G.Coloring γ :=\n    ⟨C, fun h ↦ C.valid (by simpa using h)⟩\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma ColoringOn.coe_toColoring (C : G.ColoringOn γ Set.univ) :\n    ⇑C.toColoring = ⇑C := rfl\n\n\n/-- A colouring gives rise to a partial colouring on any set -/\ndef Coloring.toColoringOn (C : G.Coloring γ) (s : Set V) : G.ColoringOn γ s :=\n    ⟨C, fun h ↦ C.valid (by simpa using h.2.2)⟩\n\n\nA graph is n-colourable if and only if G.ColorableOn n Set.univ.\n\nvariable (G)\n\n\nlemma colorableOn_univ_iff_colorable (n : ℕ) :\n    G.ColorableOn n (Set.univ : Set V) ↔ G.Colorable n := by\n  constructor <;> intro ⟨c⟩\n  · exact ⟨c.toColoring⟩\n  · exact ⟨c.toColoringOn _⟩\n\n\nThere are a few fundamental partial colourings that we will need to construct: the\npartial colouring of the empty set, the partial colouring of a singleton, and the\npartial colouring of a pair of non-adjacent vertices.\n\n/-- The partial colouring of the empty set by a colour c. -/\ndef coloringOnEmpty (c : γ) : G.ColoringOn γ ∅ :=\n  Coloring.mk (fun _ => c) fun h => h.1.elim\n\n\n/-- The partial colouring of the singleton set {a} by a colour c. -/\ndef coloringOnOfSingleton {γ : Type*} (a : V) (c : γ) :\n    G.ColoringOn γ ({a} : Set V) where\n  toFun := fun _ ↦ c\n  map_rel' := by simp\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma coloringOnOfSingleton_apply {γ : Type*} {a v : V} {c : γ} :\n  G.coloringOnOfSingleton a c v = c := rfl\n\n\n/-- The partial colouring of a pair of non-adjacent vertices by a colour c. -/\ndef coloringOnOfNotAdj {γ : Type*} {a b : V} (h : ¬ G.Adj a b) (c : γ) :\n    G.ColoringOn γ ({a, b} : Set V) where\n  toFun := fun _ ↦ c\n  map_rel' := by\n    rintro x y ⟨hx, hy, hxy⟩ -\n    aesop (add simp adj_comm)\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma coloringOnOfNotAdj_apply {γ : Type*} {a b v : V} {h : ¬ G.Adj a b} {c : γ} :\n  G.coloringOnOfNotAdj h c v = c := rfl\n\n\nvariable {G}\n\n\nWe can combine two partial colourings by extending the first using the second,\nprovided they are compatible in the sense that there is no edge between a vertex\ncoloured by C₁ and a vertex coloured only by C₂ using the same colour. This gives\na partial colouring on the union of the two sets.\n\n/-- We can combine colourings C₁ of s and C₂ of t if they are compatible i.e.\n∀ v w, v ∈ s → w ∈ t \\ s → G.Adj v w → C₁ v ≠ C₂ w to get a colouring of s ∪ t.\nIt agrees with C₁ on s and with C₂ on t \\ s. -/\ndef ColoringOn.union [DecidablePred (· ∈ s)] (C₁ : G.ColoringOn γ s)\n    (C₂ : G.ColoringOn γ t)\n    (h : ∀ ⦃v w⦄, v ∈ s → w ∈ t \\ s → G.Adj v w → C₁ v ≠ C₂ w) :\n    G.ColoringOn γ (s ∪ t) where\n  toFun := fun v ↦ ite (v ∈ s) (C₁ v) (C₂ v)\n  map_rel' := by\n    rintro v w ⟨hv', hw', hadj⟩ hf\n    split_ifs at hf with hv hw hw\n    · exact C₁.valid ⟨hv, hw, hadj⟩ hf\n    · exact h hv ⟨hw'.resolve_left hw, hw⟩ hadj hf\n    · exact h hw ⟨hv'.resolve_left hv, hv⟩ hadj.symm hf.symm\n    · exact C₂.valid ⟨hv'.resolve_left hv, hw'.resolve_left hw, hadj⟩ hf\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma ColoringOn.union_apply [DecidablePred (· ∈ s)] {v : V} (C₁ : G.ColoringOn γ s)\n    (C₂ : G.ColoringOn γ t)\n    (h : ∀ ⦃v w⦄, v ∈ s → w ∈ t \\ s → G.Adj v w → C₁ v ≠ C₂ w) :\n  (C₁.union C₂ h) v = ite (v ∈ s) (C₁ v) (C₂ v) := rfl\n\n\nThe extension of a colouring of s to insert a s using a colour c that is not\nused by C to colour a neighbour of a in s.\n\nprotected def ColoringOn.insert [DecidableEq V] (a : V) {c : γ}\n    (C : G.ColoringOn γ s) (h : ∀ ⦃v⦄, v ∈ s → G.Adj a v → C v ≠ c) :\n    G.ColoringOn γ (insert a s) :=\n  ((G.coloringOnOfSingleton a c).union C (by\n    simp only [Set.mem_singleton_iff, Set.mem_sdiff, and_imp]\n    rintro _ _ rfl h' h1 had h2\n    exact h h' had h2.symm)).copy (by simp [Set.union_comm])\n\n\nSo C.insert a h colours a with c and agrees with C elsewhere.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma ColoringOn.insert_apply [DecidableEq V] {a v : V} {c : γ}\n    (C : G.ColoringOn γ s) (h : ∀ ⦃x⦄, x ∈ s → G.Adj a x → C x ≠ c) :\n    (C.insert a h) v = ite (v = a) c (C v) := by\n  rw [ColoringOn.insert, copy_apply, union_apply]\n  simp\n\n\nFinally we can relabel the colours of a partial colouring along any injective map (in\nparticular along any permutation of the colours): validity is preserved, since an\ninjection cannot merge two distinct colours.\n\n/-- Relabel the colours of a partial colouring along an embedding. -/\ndef ColoringOn.map {β : Type*} (f : γ ↪ β) (C : G.ColoringOn γ s) :\n    G.ColoringOn β s :=\n  (Embedding.completeGraph f).toHom.comp C\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma ColoringOn.map_apply {β : Type*} (f : γ ↪ β) (C : G.ColoringOn γ s)\n    {v : V} : C.map f v = f (C v) := rfl\n\n\nend partcol\n\n\nend SimpleGraph\n\n\nnamespace SimpleGraph\nvariable {V : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V}\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Subgraphs\u0009Partial Colourings*","header":"9.6.2. Building Partial Colourings","id":"/Subgraphs/#section-building-partial-colourings"},"/Theorems/#section-mantel-s-theorem":{"contents":"The case r = 2 of Turán's theorem is Mantel's theorem: a triangle-free graph on\nn vertices has at most ⌊n² / 4⌋ edges. Equality holds for the balanced complete\nbipartite graph turanGraph n 2.\n\n#check turanNumber_two -- turanNumber n 2 = n ^ 2 / 4\n\n\nFor example, the odd cycle C[7] is triangle-free, so it has at most ⌊49 / 4⌋ = 12\nedges. (decide checks that C[7] is triangle-free using the Decidable instance\nfor CliqueFree from The Finset Of N Cliques.)\n\nexample : #C[7].edgeFinset ≤ 7 ^ 2 / 4 := by\n  rw [← turanNumber_two]\n  exact CliqueFree.card_edgeFinset_le (by decide)\n\n\nBy Triangle Free Mycielskians, the Mycielskian of a\ntriangle-free graph on n vertices is a triangle-free graph on 2n + 1 vertices, so\nMantel's theorem bounds its number of edges by ⌊(2n + 1)² / 4⌋. For the Grötzsch\ngraph mμ[C[5]] this bound is 30, while the graph has 20 edges.\n\n#eval #mμ[C[5]].edgeFinset -- 20\n\n\nThe same applies to every iterated Mycielskian mμ^n C[5]: its clique number is\nalways 2 (Clique Number Of The Mycielskian), so Mantel's theorem\nbounds its number of edges, even though its chromatic number n + 3 grows without\nbound (Colouring Mycielskians).\n\nBipartite graphs are triangle-free, so they also have at most ⌊n² / 4⌋ edges.\nMathlib states this for all bipartite graphs, using ℕ∞-valued cardinalities so\nthat no finiteness assumption is needed.\n\n#check IsBipartite.four_mul_encard_edgeSet_le\n-- G.IsBipartite → 4 * G.edgeSet.encard ≤ ENat.card V ^ 2\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Theorems\u0009Extremal Graph Theory","header":"13.2.3. Mantel's Theorem","id":"/Theorems/#section-mantel-s-theorem"},"/Walks/#section-the-bondy-chv-tal-theorem":{"contents":"The key observation of Bondy and Chvátal is that the degree condition above is only\never needed one pair at a time: if u and v are distinct non-adjacent vertices\nwith deg u + deg v ≥ |V|, then G is Hamiltonian iff G with the edge u — v\nadded is Hamiltonian. Repeatedly adding such edges yields the closure of G.\n\nWe start with an easy lemma: reversal preserves being a Hamiltonian walk.\n(Reversal also preserves being a Hamiltonian cycle, though we will not need\nthis here: see the exercises.)\n\n@[simp]\nlemma Walk.isHamiltonian_reverse [DecidableEq V] {u v : V} {p : G.Walk u v} :\n    p.reverse.IsHamiltonian ↔ p.IsHamiltonian := by\n  simp only [Walk.IsHamiltonian, support_reverse, List.count_reverse]\n\n\nThe first step towards the Bondy–Chvátal theorem is a piece of cycle surgery: given a\nHamiltonian cycle c through an edge u — v, deleting that edge from c should\nleave a Hamiltonian path from u to v.\n\nWe first need to prove a simple lemma about darts of a cycle.\n\n#check darts_getElem_eq_getVert\n\n\nIf d is a dart of a cycle c : G.Walk u u and the first vertex of d is u, then\nthe second vertex of d is the second vertex of the cycle.\n\nlemma Walk.IsCycle.snd_eq_of_mem_darts {u : V} {c : G.Walk u u}\n    (hc : c.IsCycle) {d : G.Dart} (hd : d ∈ c.darts) (hfst : d.fst = u) :\n    d.snd = c.snd := by\n  obtain ⟨i, hi, rfl⟩ := List.mem_iff_getElem.1 hd\n  rw [darts_getElem_eq_getVert] at hfst ⊢\n  have hilt : i < c.length := c.length_darts ▸ hi\n  -- the dart leaves u, which only occurs at the ends, so its index is 0\n  have h0 : i = 0 := by\n    rcases (hc.getVert_endpoint_iff hilt.le).1 hfst with h | h <;> grind\n  subst h0\n  rfl\n\n\nCutting a Hamiltonian cycle at its first edge yields a Hamiltonian path: the reversed\ntail runs from the endpoint u to the second vertex, visits every vertex, and avoids\nthe deleted first edge (a cycle is a trail, so no edge is repeated).\n\nlemma Walk.IsHamiltonianCycle.exists_isHamiltonian_walk_snd [DecidableEq V] {u : V}\n    {c : G.Walk u u} (hc : c.IsHamiltonianCycle) :\n    ∃ p : G.Walk u c.snd, p.IsHamiltonian ∧ s(u, c.snd) ∉ p.edges := by\n  refine ⟨c.tail.reverse, isHamiltonian_reverse.2 hc.isHamiltonian_tail, ?_⟩\n  rw [edges_reverse, List.mem_reverse]\n  have hnd : c.edges.Nodup := hc.isCycle.edges_nodup\n  rw [← c.cons_tail_eq hc.isCycle.not_nil, edges_cons] at hnd\n  exact (List.nodup_cons.1 hnd).1\n\n\nNow the general surgery lemma: a Hamiltonian cycle through an edge u — v (based\nanywhere) can be cut open into a Hamiltonian path from u to v avoiding u — v.\nThe edge u — v of the cycle is the image of a dart, which either points from u\nto v or from v to u (dart_edge_eq_mk'_iff'). Rotating the cycle to be\nbased at the dart's first vertex makes the dart the first dart of the cycle,\nso cutting at the first edge yields a Hamiltonian path from the dart's first vertex\nto its second. This is the required path in the first case, and its reverse is the\nrequired path in the second.\n\nlemma Walk.IsHamiltonianCycle.exists_isHamiltonian_walk_of_mem_edges [DecidableEq V]\n    {x u v : V} {c : G.Walk x x} (hc : c.IsHamiltonianCycle)\n    (he : s(u, v) ∈ c.edges) :\n    ∃ p : G.Walk u v, p.IsHamiltonian ∧ s(u, v) ∉ p.edges := by\n  -- cutting c open at a dart from a to b gives a Hamiltonian a-b path\n  have key : ∀ {a b : V}, ∀ d ∈ c.darts, d.fst = a → d.snd = b →\n      ∃ p : G.Walk a b, p.IsHamiltonian ∧ s(a, b) ∉ p.edges := by\n    intro a b d hd hfst hsnd\n    have ha := hc.mem_support a\n    have hb : (c.rotate a ha).snd = b :=\n      hsnd ▸ ((hc.rotate ha).isCycle.snd_eq_of_mem_darts\n        ((c.rotate_darts a ha).perm.mem_iff.2 hd) hfst).symm\n    obtain ⟨p, h1, h2⟩ := (hc.rotate ha).exists_isHamiltonian_walk_snd\n    exact ⟨p.copy rfl hb, (isHamiltonian_copy ..).2 h1, by simpa [← hb] using h2⟩\n  -- the edge u — v is the image of a dart of c\n  obtain ⟨d, hd, hde⟩ := List.mem_map.1 he\n  rcases dart_edge_eq_mk'_iff'.1 hde with ⟨hfst, hsnd⟩ | ⟨hfst, hsnd⟩\n  · -- the dart points from u to v: cut there\n    exact key d hd hfst hsnd\n  · -- the dart points from v to u: cut there and reverse the resulting path\n    obtain ⟨p, h1, h2⟩ := key d hd hfst hsnd\n    exact ⟨p.reverse, isHamiltonian_reverse.2 h1, by simpa [Sym2.eq_swap] using h2⟩\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Walks\u0009Hamiltonian Graphs: Ore, Dirac and Bondy–Chvátal*","header":"8.8.4. The Bondy–Chvátal Theorem","id":"/Walks/#section-the-bondy-chv-tal-theorem"}});